PLACES=[
# ---- the north: York, Chester and Lancaster counties ----
N('landsford-canal-sc','Landsford Canal','Catawba','sc','canal','ruins',34.77889,-80.87823,'site','public','walk','canal','1823–c.1840',
  'A two-mile canal with five locks, opened in 1823 to carry boats around the rapids of the Catawba River and designed by Robert Mills. It never paid: one lock collapsed in 1824 on a poor foundation, and traffic had apparently stopped by 1840. The granite locks and a lock keeper\'s house survive; the house was moved here from the Rocky Mount Canal downstream near Great Falls. Listed on the National Register in 1969.',
  'The 1.5-mile Canal Trail follows the canal remains. The ranger\'s favourite spot, the Davie Mill site about a mile down the trail, lets you walk on the canal\'s earthen floor between two immense stone walls. The park also has a 1790 log cabin. In May and June the rocky shoals spider lilies flower in the river beside it.',
  'A Robert Mills canal of granite locks, left in the woods by the Catawba.',
  'Landsford Canal State Park (South Carolina State Parks). Open daily, daylight until dark; office 11 a.m. to noon; admission $6 adults, less for seniors and children; pets on a leash. Park at the main lot on Park Drive, at the start of the Canal Trail. Hours from the park\'s own page, November 2026.',
  [W('Landsford Canal'),P('Landsford Canal','landsford-canal')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours='Daily, daylight until dark',th=[34.7912,-80.88187],thn='Park lot on Park Drive'),
N('historic-brattonsville','Historic Brattonsville','McConnells','sc','town','standing',34.86513,-81.1749,'site','open','walk','','1776–1855',
  'The Bratton family\'s plantation village in York County, now an open-air museum. It has three houses built between 1776 and 1855: the Revolutionary House, begun by Colonel William Bratton in 1776 as a one-room log house; the Homestead of about 1830, built as a doctor\'s home for the family; and the Brick House of 1855, first a boarding school for girls. In 2021 a restored building opened an exhibition on Reconstruction and the African American community at Brattonsville, 1865–1877. Scenes of the film The Patriot (2000) were shot here.',
  'The Brick House has a two-story brick front with end chimneys and a two-tiered portico on stuccoed brick columns. Guided tours Tuesday to Saturday at 11 a.m. and 2 p.m.; the burial ground closes at 4 p.m. Interiors of the houses not checked here.',
  'A plantation village from the Revolution to 1855, with the history of the people enslaved there.',
  'Run by the Culture & Heritage Museums of York County. Adults $12, seniors $10, youth $8. Visitor centre and parking off Brattonsville Road (from Hwy 322 turn onto Brattonsville Road); the road is closed for some events and a detour is posted. Pets not allowed. Closed on major holidays. Hours from the museums\' own page, November 2026.',
  [W('Brattonsville Historic District'),L('Culture & Heritage Museums: plan your visit','https://chmuseums.org/?p=673')],
  conf='',brick='some',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sunday 1 to 5 p.m.; closed Monday',th=[34.86503,-81.17383],thn='Visitor centre lot'),
N('lancaster-county-courthouse-sc','Lancaster County Courthouse','Lancaster','sc','civic','standing',34.72003,-80.77103,'exact','view','drive','','1828',
  'A two-story courthouse of locally made brick laid in English bond, built in 1828 by Willis Alsobrook; its design has been credited, without firm proof, to Robert Mills, who designed the county\'s old 1823 jail. It is a National Historic Landmark (1973). Wikipedia says it was the scene of the last witch trials in the United States. Arson badly damaged it in 2008; it was restored by 2011.',
  'The five-bay front, with paired stairs up to a temple portico on Tuscan columns and windows set in round arches. Inside, the ground floor has barrel vaults and the courtroom a carved Federal judge\'s bench. Wikipedia says both that it has been in use since 1828 and that it became a museum after the 2011 restoration; whether it is open and when was not checked.',
  'A Robert Mills-style courthouse that came back from an arson fire.',
  'County building at the southwest corner of Main Street (US 521) and Meeting Street (SC 9) in downtown Lancaster; street parking. Seen from the street; opening not checked.',
  [W('Lancaster County Courthouse (South Carolina)')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',hours=''),
# ---- the west: Newberry, Abbeville, Pendleton ----
N('newberry-opera-house','Newberry Opera House','Newberry','sc','civic','standing',34.27452,-81.62094,'exact','open','drive','','1881–',
  'A French Gothic building completed in 1881 and dedicated in 1882, built as both the town hall and a theatre: the ground floor held the council chamber, offices, three jail cells and the fire engine, the upper floor a 426-seat hall. Touring companies played here for years; it later showed films, the last in 1952. Restoration began in 1998, with an addition that lets it stage full productions, and it is a working theatre again. Listed on the National Register in 1969.',
  'The 130 ft clock tower, topped by a garfish weather vane. To see the hall, go to a performance. Interior not checked.',
  'A town hall, jail and fire house with an opera house upstairs.',
  'Run by the Newberry Opera House, a non-profit, at 1201 McKibben Street. The hours given are the box office\'s, from its own page (November 2026); performances are on its calendar.',
  [W('Newberry Opera House'),L('Newberry Opera House: theater policies','https://www.newberryoperahouse.com/?p=476')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Box office Tuesday to Friday 9 a.m. to 5:30 p.m., Saturday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('abbeville-opera-house','Abbeville Opera House','Abbeville','sc','civic','standing',34.17762,-82.3779,'exact','open','drive','','1904–',
  'A theatre designed by William Augustus Edwards, opened in 1904 and dedicated in 1908 as part of the new Abbeville County Courthouse and City Hall; it is also called the Opera House and Municipal Office Building. Abbeville was an overnight stop for touring companies on the New York to Atlanta circuit, and townspeople built the hall so they could stage the shows passing through. It is the state\'s official Rural Drama State Theatre and was listed on the National Register in 1970.',
  'The opera house and municipal building on the square, within the Abbeville Historic District. To see the hall, go to a performance. Interior not checked.',
  'Where touring shows stopped for the night between New York and Atlanta.',
  'A working theatre on East Pickens Street off the town square; street parking. Its own page could not be read when this was written, so hours and the performance calendar were not checked.',
  [W('Abbeville Opera House')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('burt-stark-mansion','Burt-Stark Mansion','Abbeville','sc','landmark','standing',34.18107,-82.38241,'exact','open','drive','','1830s',
  'A house built in the 1830s by David Lesley, an Abbeville lawyer, judge and planter. Armistead Burt owned it in 1865, and on May 2 of that year Jefferson Davis held his last council of war in it: his cabinet officers were unanimously against carrying on the fight, and Davis saw the Confederate cause was lost. The Stark family bought it in 1900 and restored it; the last of them gave it to the Abbeville County Historic Preservation Commission. A National Historic Landmark since 1992.',
  'The house and its rooms, shown by tour. Interior not checked here.',
  'The parlour where the Confederacy\'s last council of war gave up.',
  'Owned by the Abbeville County Historic Preservation Commission, at 400 North Main Street. The state tourism office\'s article gives tours Friday and Saturday 1 to 5 p.m. or by appointment, $10 a person; the commission\'s own page could not be read, so hours are not confirmed.',
  [W('Burt-Stark Mansion'),L('Discover South Carolina: historic Burt-Stark Mansion','https://discoversouthcarolina.com/articles/historic-burt-stark-mansion')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('ashtabula-pendleton','Ashtabula','Pendleton','sc','landmark','standing',34.67889,-82.75694,'site','open','drive','','c.1825',
  'A two-story clapboard plantation house built in the mid-1820s for Lewis Ladson Gibbes of Charleston, whose wife Maria Drayton came from Drayton Hall; both died before it was finished. About 1790 Thomas Lofton had built a brick house on the site, later run as a tavern on the stage road. From 1837 Dr. Ozey R. Broyles farmed it and invented a subsurface plow here. It is part of the Pendleton Historic District, listed on the National Register in 1972.',
  'A Lowcountry-style house built for a Charleston family in the Upstate, on ten acres of open ground. A docent shows the house and its furnishings; there is no modern heating or cooling, so dress for the weather.',
  'A Charleston planter\'s summer house, three miles out of Pendleton.',
  'Pendleton Historic Foundation, at 2725 Old Greenville Highway (SC 88) about three miles east of Pendleton. Walk-up tours $10 adults, $5 ages 5 to 18, cash or cheque only. The foundation also shows Woodburn, Thursday and Friday 10 a.m. to 1 p.m. and Sunday at 3:30 p.m. Hours from the foundation\'s own page, November 2026.',
  [W('Ashtabula (Pendleton, South Carolina)'),L('Pendleton Historic Foundation: Ashtabula','https://pendletonhistoricfoundation.org/ashtabula')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours='Wednesday to Friday 10 a.m. to 1 p.m.; Sunday one tour at 2 p.m.'),
# ---- the Midlands and the Pee Dee ----
N('holy-cross-stateburg','Church of the Holy Cross, Stateburg','Stateburg','sc','worship','standing',33.95333,-80.53203,'exact','view','drive','','1850–1852',
  'A Gothic Revival church built in 1850–52 of rammed earth, to a design by Edward C. Jones, on land given earlier by General Thomas Sumter. The building committee was chaired by Dr. William Wallace Anderson, whose Borough House across the road is also of rammed earth. It is a National Historic Landmark (1973). The congregation left the Episcopal Church in 2013 for the Anglican Church in North America.',
  'Walls of yellow rammed earth 40 ft tall, covered in stucco, with buttresses, a cruciform plan, an attached tower, a steep tile roof and Gothic-arched windows. Inside, the walls are painted grey to look like stone (Wikipedia). Visiting hours not checked.',
  'A tall Gothic church built of rammed earth.',
  'An active Anglican church at 335 North Kings Highway, west of Sumter. No service times were found on a page that could be read; look from the road and churchyard, and see the parish site for services.',
  [W('Church of the Holy Cross (Stateburg, South Carolina)'),L('Church of the Holy Cross, Stateburg','https://holycrossstateburg.com/wp/?p=6')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('old-horry-county-courthouse','Old Horry County Courthouse (Conway City Hall)','Conway','sc','civic','standing',33.83497,-79.04613,'exact','view','drive','','1824–1825',
  'A two-story Classical Revival brick courthouse built in 1824–25 and said to be a design of Robert Mills. It is now Conway City Hall. Listed on the National Register in 1971.',
  'The extended pediment on Doric columns, sheltering a second-floor portico that does not run the full width of the front. City hall is a working office; interior not checked.',
  'Robert Mills\'s courthouse for Horry County, now the city hall.',
  'City of Conway, at 1001 Third Avenue in downtown Conway; street parking. Seen from the street; office hours not checked.',
  [W('Old Horry County Courthouse')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours=''),
N('hebron-church-bucksville','Hebron Church','Bucksville','sc','worship','standing',33.71553,-79.06783,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','c.1855',
  'A Methodist meeting-house-form church of about 1855 at Bucksville, a one-story frame building with batten siding and a tin-covered gable roof. Its churchyard and the Henry Buck family graveyard across the road come with it. Listed on the National Register in 1977.',
  'The pedimented portico on five square wooden columns, and the two graveyards. Whether services are held and the interior were not checked.',
  'A plain board-and-batten meeting house among the Bucksville graves.',
  'On Bucksville Drive, Bucksville, south of Conway. Owner and services not checked: look from the road and keep to the graveyards with respect.',
  [W('Hebron Church (Bucksville, South Carolina)')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours=''),
# ---- the Lowcountry ----
N('hopsewee-plantation','Hopsewee','Georgetown','sc','landmark','standing',33.21074,-79.38458,'exact','open','drive','','c.1735–1740',
  'The house of a Santee River rice plantation and the birthplace of Thomas Lynch Jr., a signer of the Declaration of Independence. Wikipedia dates it to 1735; the owners\' page says about 1740. The Lynches sold it in 1752 to Robert Hume, whose family held it until at least 1853; like many Santee plantations it was abandoned in the Civil War. The enslaved people who worked the rice fields are part of the tour.',
  'The owners say the house keeps much of its original woodwork and glass: wide centre halls, hand-carved mouldings and random-width heart-pine floors, furnished with 18th and 19th century pieces. Tours take 50 minutes; no photographs inside.',
  'A rice planter\'s house on the Santee where a signer of the Declaration was born.',
  'Privately owned and shown by tour, at 494 Hopsewee Road off US 17 between Georgetown and McClellanville. Adults $24, seniors $22, youth $15, children $10; a tea room on the grounds. Tour days were not given on the page read; check the owners\' site.',
  [W('Hopsewee'),L('Hopsewee: plantation tour','https://hopsewee.com/phone/tour-historic-southern-plantation.html')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('drayton-hall-sc','Drayton Hall','Charleston','sc','landmark','standing',32.87091,-80.07625,'exact','open','drive','','1747–1750s',
  'A Palladian plantation house on the Ashley River, built for John Drayton, and the only plantation house on the river to come through both the Revolution and the Civil War intact. It was long dated 1738 to 1752, but tree-ring dating in 2014 found its attic timbers were cut in the winter of 1747–48, so it was probably lived in by the early 1750s. A National Historic Landmark (1960).',
  'The two-storey portico on the land side, modelled on Palladio, and inside a central stair hall with a divided staircase and chimney-pieces after Inigo Jones. The house is kept rather than restored, so later changes and wear survive. Guided house tours of an hour on the hour from 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.',
  'The oldest preserved plantation house in America still open to the public, its owners say.',
  'Run by Drayton Hall, 3380 Ashley River Road. All-inclusive tickets $30 online, $35 at the gate (no cash). Free parking. Closed Tuesdays and on Thanksgiving, Christmas Eve and Day, New Year\'s Eve and Day. Hours from its own page, November 2026.',
  [W('Drayton Hall'),L('Drayton Hall: visit','https://www.draytonhall.org/visit/')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Wednesday to Monday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. (gate closes 3 p.m.); closed Tuesday'),
N('powder-magazine-charleston','Powder Magazine','Charleston','sc','fort','standing',32.77944,-79.93,'exact','view','walk','','1713',
  'A gunpowder store finished in 1713, authorised in 1703 during Queen Anne\'s War: the oldest surviving public building of the old Province of Carolina. It held powder through the Revolution. The National Society of the Colonial Dames of America in South Carolina bought it in 1902 and ran it as a museum, but closed it to general visitors on February 28, 2025, keeping it for school groups and planned events (Charleston City Paper).',
  'A square single-storey building with stuccoed brick walls about three feet thick at the base, a large arch in each wall, and its original red tile roof, pyramidal with crossing gables, built so that an explosion would go up through the roof. The cannon yard is in front.',
  'Charleston\'s oldest public building, made to blow its roof off rather than its walls.',
  'Owned by the Colonial Dames, at 79 Cumberland Street between Church and Meeting streets. Closed to general admission since February 2025; seen from the street. Paid parking in downtown Charleston.',
  [W('Powder Magazine (Charleston, South Carolina)'),L('Charleston City Paper: Colonial Dames partially close historic Powder Magazine (2025)','https://charlestoncitypaper.com/2025/03/06/colonial-dames-partially-close-historic-powder-magazine')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours=''),
N('old-exchange-charleston','Old Exchange and Provost Dungeon','Charleston','sc','civic','standing',32.77685,-79.92687,'exact','open','walk','','1767–1771',
  'The Exchange and Custom House at the foot of Broad Street, built in 1767–71. In the Revolution the British held prisoners of war in its cellar, the Provost Dungeon. South Carolina\'s convention to ratify the Constitution met here in 1788 and many events of George Washington\'s week in Charleston took place here. Enslaved people were sold on the plaza north of it, as an 1853 painting shows. A National Historic Landmark (1973), now a museum of the Daughters of the American Revolution.',
  'A two-storey building on a high brick basement, with a cupola, a three-bay pedimented centre with round-arched entrances, Ionic pilasters and a Palladian window at each side. Admission includes the upper floors\' exhibition and a 25-minute guided tour of the dungeon every half hour from 10 a.m.',
  'A colonial custom house with the Revolution\'s prison in its cellar.',
  'At 122 East Bay Street, at Broad Street. Adults $16, children 7 to 12 $12; lift to all floors. Closed New Year\'s Day, Thanksgiving and Christmas. Paid parking in downtown Charleston. Hours from its own page, November 2026.',
  [W('Exchange and Provost'),L('Old Exchange: plan a visit','https://www.oldexchange.org/?p=1195')],
  conf='',brick='?',hours='Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sunday 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('pon-pon-chapel-ruins','Pon Pon Chapel ruins (the Burnt Church)','Jacksonboro','sc','worship','ruins',32.8087,-80.48941,'exact','public','walk','cemeteries,burned','1753–1832',
  'The chapel of ease of St. Bartholomew\'s Parish, on the old stage road from Charleston to Savannah, provided for by the General Assembly in 1725; John Wesley preached here in April 1737. The vestry ordered a brick building in 1753 to replace the wooden chapel. The markers disagree on its fires: one says it burned between 1796 and 1806, another in 1801, a third during the Revolution, rebuilt between 1819 and 1822 and burned again in 1832; Wikipedia gives 1820 as the building date. It was never rebuilt, and has been called the Burnt Church since.',
  'The walls of the chapel, repaired with a state grant in 1975, and the old cemetery, where O\'Brien Smith of Duharra Plantation and Aedanus Burke are buried. The ruins stand on Parker\'s Ferry Road, a barely paved road along a power line, just north-east of its junction with Jacksonboro Road (S-15-40).',
  'A brick chapel where Wesley preached, burned and left roofless.',
  'The chapel and four acres were deeded to the Colleton County Historical Society in 1970 (HMdb marker text). Park on the shoulder of Parker\'s Ferry Road by the markers. No hours found; daylight visits.',
  [W('Pon Pon Chapel'),L('HMdb: Ruins of Pon Pon Chapel of Ease','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=7120'),L('HMdb: Pon Pon Chapel (Colleton County Historical Society)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=66489'),L('HMdb: Pon Pon Chapel roadside marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=7073')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours=''),
N('rivers-bridge-sc','Rivers Bridge battlefield earthworks','Ehrhardt','sc','fort','traces',33.05653,-81.09416,'area','public','walk','forts','1865',
  'In February 1865 Confederate troops held this ground by the Salkahatchie River for two days against General Sherman\'s army marching north from Savannah, until they were outflanked and withdrew. In 1876 local men reburied the Confederate dead in a mass grave about a mile from the field. The Rivers Bridge Memorial Association later bought the battlefield and gave it to the state in 1945. Listed on the National Register in 1972.',
  'Still-intact earthen fortifications, which the state parks call among the best preserved Civil War works in South Carolina. A 0.56-mile Battlefield Trail with panels ends at a cannon platform; ranger tours go to the earthworks. The Confederate cemetery and memorial are on the grounds.',
  'Confederate earthworks by the Salkahatchie, the one preserved battlefield in the state park system.',
  'Battle of Rivers Bridge State Historic Site (South Carolina State Parks), 390 acres. Open daily 9 a.m. to sunset; office 11 a.m. to noon. Park admission free; Saturday tours $5 adults. Pets on a leash. Hours from the park\'s own page, November 2026.',
  [W('Rivers Bridge State Historic Site'),P('Battle of Rivers Bridge','rivers-bridge')],
  conf='',brick='no',hours='Daily 9 a.m. to sunset'),
]

